    </p>

</section>


<div class="container">


<!-- WHAT IS CODING DECODING -->

<section class="section">

    <h2>📚 What is Coding-Decoding?</h2>

    <p>
        Coding-Decoding is a Reasoning topic in which a word,
        number or group of letters is represented using a
        particular code or pattern.
    </p>

    <p>
        In SSC examinations, you are usually given one or more
        examples of coded information. You have to identify the
        underlying rule and apply the same rule to another word,
        letter or number.
    </p>

    <div class="highlight">
        Coding-Decoding = Identify the Code Rule → Apply the Rule → Find the Answer
    </div>

</section>


<!-- BASIC CONCEPT -->

<section class="section">

    <h2>🎯 Basic Concept</h2>

    <p>
        Consider the following example:
    </p>

    <div class="code-box">
        <strong>CAT → DBU</strong>
    </div>

    <p>
        Compare each letter:
    </p>

    <div class="code-box">
        C → D &nbsp;&nbsp; A → B &nbsp;&nbsp; T → U
    </div>

    <p>
        Every letter is moved one position forward in the
        English alphabet.
    </p>

    <div class="highlight">
        C + 1 = D &nbsp;&nbsp; A + 1 = B &nbsp;&nbsp; T + 1 = U
    </div>

    <p>
        Therefore, if the same rule is applied to another word,
        its code can be determined.
    </p>

</section>


<!-- ALPHABET POSITION -->

<section class="section">

    <h2>🔤 Alphabet Position</h2>

    <p>
        Alphabet positions are extremely important in
        Coding-Decoding questions.
    </p>

    <div class="highlight">
        A = 1, B = 2, C = 3, ... Z = 26
    </div>

    <h3>Example</h3>

    <div class="code-box">
        A = 1 &nbsp;&nbsp;
        B = 2 &nbsp;&nbsp;
        C = 3 &nbsp;&nbsp;
        D = 4
    </div>

    <p>
        If a coding rule is based on alphabet positions,
        convert the letters into their numerical positions
        before solving.
    </p>

</section>


<!-- TYPES -->

<section class="section">

    <h2>📖 Types of Coding-Decoding Questions</h2>

    <div class="cards">

        <div class="card">
            <h3>1. Letter Coding</h3>
            <p>
                Letters are replaced or shifted according to
                a particular rule.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
            <h3>2. Number Coding</h3>
            <p>
                Words or letters are represented using numbers
                or numerical relationships.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
            <h3>3. Mixed Coding</h3>
            <p>
                Letters, numbers and symbols may be combined
                in the coding pattern.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
            <h3>4. Substitution Coding</h3>
            <p>
                One word or object is represented by another
                word according to the given information.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
            <h3>5. Conditional Coding</h3>
            <p>
                Different conditions determine how particular
                letters or numbers are coded.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
            <h3>6. Chinese Coding</h3>
            <p>
                Words are represented using coded words and
                the common code is identified through comparison.
            </p>
        </div>

    </div>

</section>


<!-- LETTER CODING -->

<section class="section">

    <h2>🔠 Letter Coding</h2>

    <p>
        In letter coding, each letter is changed according to
        a specific alphabetic relationship.
    </p>

    <h3>Example 1 – Forward Shift</h3>

    <div class="code-box">
        CAT → DBU
    </div>

    <p>
        Each letter moves one position forward.
    </p>

    <p>
        Therefore:
    </p>

    <div class="code-box">
        DOG → EPH
    </div>

    <p>
        D → E, O → P, G → H
    </p>


    <h3>Example 2 – Backward Shift</h3>

    <div class="code-box">
        DOG → CNF
    </div>

    <p>
        Each letter moves one position backward.
    </p>

    <p>
        D → C, O → N, G → F
    </p>

</section>


<!-- POSITION BASED -->

<section class="section">

    <h2>🔢 Position-Based Coding</h2>

    <p>
        Sometimes letters are converted into their numerical
        positions.
    </p>

    <div class="example">

        <strong>Example:</strong>

        <p>
            CAT
        </p>

        <p>
            C = 3, A = 1, T = 20
        </p>

        <p>
            Therefore:
            <strong>CAT = 3, 1, 20</strong>
        </p>

    </div>

    <p>
        In some questions, these positions may then be added,
        subtracted, multiplied or transformed according to
        the given rule.
    </p>

</section>


<!-- REVERSE ALPHABET -->

<section class="section">

    <h2>🔄 Reverse Alphabet Coding</h2>

    <p>
        Some Coding-Decoding questions use reverse alphabet
        positions.
    </p>

    <div class="highlight">
        A ↔ Z &nbsp;&nbsp;
        B ↔ Y &nbsp;&nbsp;
        C ↔ X &nbsp;&nbsp;
        D ↔ W
    </div>

    <p>
        The reverse position can be calculated using:
    </p>

    <div class="code-box">
        Reverse Position = 27 − Forward Position
    </div>

    <h3>Example</h3>

    <p>
        C = 3
    </p>

    <p>
        Reverse position = 27 − 3 = 24
    </p>

    <p>
        Therefore C corresponds to X.
    </p>

</section>


<!-- NUMBER CODING -->

<section class="section">

    <h2>🔢 Number Coding</h2>

    <p>
        In Number Coding, words or letters may be represented
        through numbers.
    </p>

    <div class="example">

        <strong>Example:</strong>

        <p>
            If CAT is coded as 24:
        </p>

        <p>
            C = 3, A = 1, T = 20
        </p>

        <p>
            3 + 1 + 20 = 24
        </p>

        <div class="highlight">
            CAT → 24
        </div>

    </div>

    <p>
        Always check whether the numbers are related to
        alphabet positions or another mathematical operation.
    </p>

</section>


<!-- MIXED CODING -->

<section class="section">

    <h2>🔢🔤 Mixed Coding</h2>

    <p>
        Mixed Coding may involve alphabets, numbers and symbols
        together.
    </p>

    <div class="code-box">
        A1, B2, C3, D4
    </div>

    <p>
        Here each letter corresponds to its alphabet position.
    </p>

    <p>
        Therefore:
    </p>

    <div class="code-box">
        E5, F6, G7, H8
    </div>

</section>


<!-- SUBSTITUTION -->

<section class="section">

    <h2>🔁 Substitution Coding</h2>

    <p>
        In substitution coding, one object, word or person
        is represented by another word.
    </p>

    <div class="example">

        <p>
            If:
        </p>

        <div class="code-box">
            Pen is called Book
            <br>
            Book is called Table
            <br>
            Table is called Chair
        </div>

        <p>
            Then the question may ask:
        </p>

        <p>
            What is used for writing?
        </p>

        <p>
            Normally we use a Pen, but according to the code,
            Pen is called <strong>Book</strong>.
        </p>

        <div class="highlight">
            Answer = Book
        </div>

    </div>

</section>


<!-- CONDITIONAL CODING -->

<section class="section">

    <h2>⚙️ Conditional Coding</h2>

    <p>
        In conditional coding, different rules are applied
        depending on the given conditions.
    </p>

    <h3>Example Conditions</h3>

    <ul>

        <li>
            If the letter is a vowel, move it one position forward.
        </li>

        <li>
            If the letter is a consonant, move it one position backward.
        </li>

        <li>
            If the number is even, add 2.
        </li>

        <li>
            If the number is odd, subtract 1.
        </li>

    </ul>

    <p>
        Always read every condition carefully before solving
        the question.
    </p>

</section>


<!-- CHINESE CODING -->

<section class="section">

    <h2>🧩 Chinese Coding</h2>

    <p>
        Chinese Coding questions provide several statements
        in which words are represented by coded words.
    </p>

    <p>
        The common code is identified by comparing the statements.
    </p>

    <div class="example">

        <p>
            Suppose:
        </p>

        <div class="code-box">
            red apple = ka la
            <br>
            red ball = ka ma
        </div>

        <p>
            The common word is <strong>red</strong>.
        </p>

        <p>
            The common code is <strong>ka</strong>.
        </p>

        <div class="highlight">
            red → ka
        </div>

    </div>

</section>


<!-- HOW TO SOLVE -->

<section class="section">

    <h2>🧠 How to Solve Coding-Decoding Questions</h2>

    <h3>Step 1 – Read the Given Information</h3>

    <p>
        Carefully read all coded examples before attempting
        the question.
    </p>

    <h3>Step 2 – Compare the Elements</h3>

    <p>
        Compare letters, numbers or words to identify what
        changes and what remains constant.
    </p>

    <h3>Step 3 – Check Alphabet Positions</h3>

    <p>
        Convert letters into positions whenever required.
    </p>

    <h3>Step 4 – Identify the Rule</h3>

    <p>
        Look for forward shift, backward shift, reverse
        alphabet, addition, subtraction or substitution.
    </p>

    <h3>Step 5 – Apply the Rule</h3>

    <p>
        Apply the identified rule to the word or element asked
        in the question.
    </p>

    <h3>Step 6 – Verify</h3>

    <p>
        Check the rule against the original examples before
        selecting the final answer.
    </p>

    <div class="highlight">
        Read → Compare → Find Rule → Apply → Verify → Answer
    </div>

</section>


<!-- IMPORTANT POINTS -->

<section class="section">

    <h2>⭐ Important Points for SSC</h2>

    <ul>

        <li>
            Remember alphabet positions from A = 1 to Z = 26.
        </li>

        <li>
            Check both forward and backward alphabet movement.
        </li>

        <li>
            Look for reverse alphabet relationships when required.
        </li>

        <li>
            Compare each letter separately before assuming
            the complete rule.
        </li>

        <li>
            In number coding, check whether numbers represent
            alphabet positions.
        </li>

        <li>
            In substitution questions, follow the given names
            exactly instead of using their normal meanings.
        </li>

        <li>
            In conditional coding, apply every condition in
            the correct order.
        </li>

        <li>
            In Chinese Coding, compare common words and common
            codes across statements.
        </li>

        <li>
            Do not assume a coding rule without verifying it
            against all given information.
        </li>

        <li>
            Regular practice improves speed and accuracy.
        </li>

    </ul>

</section>


<!-- PRACTICE QUESTIONS -->

<section class="section">

    <h2>📝 SSC-Level Practice Questions</h2>


    <div class="practice">

        <strong>Q1. If CAT is coded as DBU, how is DOG coded?</strong>

        <p>
            A) EPH &nbsp;&nbsp;
            B) EOG &nbsp;&nbsp;
            C) FPH &nbsp;&nbsp;
            D) DPH
        </p>

        <div class="answer">
            Answer: A) EPH
        </div>

    </div>


    <div class="practice">

        <strong>
            Q2. If each letter is moved one position backward,
            how is DOG coded?
        </strong>

        <p>
            A) CNF &nbsp;&nbsp;
            B) EPH &nbsp;&nbsp;
            C) COG &nbsp;&nbsp;
            D) BME
        </p>

        <div class="answer">
            Answer: A) CNF
        </div>

    </div>


    <div class="practice">

        <strong>
            Q3. If A = 1, B = 2, ... Z = 26,
            what is the value of CAT?
        </strong>

        <p>
            A) 22 &nbsp;&nbsp;
            B) 24 &nbsp;&nbsp;
            C) 26 &nbsp;&nbsp;
            D) 28
        </p>

        <div class="answer">
            Answer: B) 24
        </div>

    </div>


    <div class="practice">

        <strong>
            Q4. If A is coded as Z, B as Y and C as X,
            how is D coded?
        </strong>

        <p>
            A) V &nbsp;&nbsp;
            B) W &nbsp;&nbsp;
            C) X &nbsp;&nbsp;
            D) Y
        </p>

        <div class="answer">
            Answer: B) W
        </div>

    </div>


    <div class="practice">

        <strong>
            Q5. If PEN is coded as QFO by moving every letter
            one position forward, how is BOOK coded?
        </strong>

        <p>
            A) CPPL &nbsp;&nbsp;
            B) CPPK &nbsp;&nbsp;
            C) APPL &nbsp;&nbsp;
            D) CQPL
        </p>

        <div class="answer">
            Answer: A) CPPL
        </div>

    </div>


    <div class="practice">

        <strong>
            Q6. If RED APPLE is coded as KA LA and
            RED BALL is coded as KA MA, what is the code for RED?
        </strong>

        <p>
            A) LA &nbsp;&nbsp;
            B) KA &nbsp;&nbsp;
            C) MA &nbsp;&nbsp;
            D) RA
        </p>

        <div class="answer">
            Answer: B) KA
        </div>

    </div>


    <div class="practice">

        <strong>
            Q7. If A = 1, B = 2, C = 3, what is the code for D?
        </strong>

        <p>
            A) 2 &nbsp;&nbsp;
            B) 3 &nbsp;&nbsp;
            C) 4 &nbsp;&nbsp;
            D) 5
        </p>

        <div class="answer">
            Answer: C) 4
        </div>

    </div>


    <div class="practice">

        <strong>
            Q8. If SUN is coded as TVO by moving every letter
            one position forward, how is MOON coded?
        </strong>

        <p>
            A) NPPO &nbsp;&nbsp;
            B) NPPM &nbsp;&nbsp;
            C) LNNM &nbsp;&nbsp;
            D) NQPO
        </p>

        <div class="answer">
            Answer: A) NPPO
        </div>

    </div>


</section>


<!-- QUICK REVISION -->

<section class="section">

    <h2>⚡ Quick Revision</h2>

    <ul>

        <li>
            Coding-Decoding = finding a hidden rule used to
            represent letters, numbers or words.
        </li>

        <li>
            Alphabet positions: A = 1 to Z = 26.
        </li>

        <li>
            Check forward and backward letter shifts.
        </li>

        <li>
            Reverse alphabet: A ↔ Z, B ↔ Y, C ↔ X.
        </li>

        <li>
            Number Coding may use alphabet positions or
            mathematical operations.
        </li>

        <li>
            Substitution Coding requires following the given
            replacement names.
        </li>

        <li>
            Conditional Coding requires careful application
            of every condition.
        </li>

        <li>
            Chinese Coding is solved by comparing common words
            and common codes.
        </li>

        <li>
            Always verify the identified rule before answering.
        </li>

    </ul>

</section>


